  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-81769",
    "knowledge_point_id": "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction",
    "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=1-2t$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=-6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right.",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =(4)-(4)^{2}+C \\\\ -6 & =4-16+C \\\\ -6 & =-12+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+6.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-{t}^{2}+t+6=-({t}^{2}-t-6)=-(t+2)(t-3)>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/74db16dc00f5d08637cef02960409b7fd15c4bd3c19f577fa5a1f30ac8c402d3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<3.$",
